timbuk2 a legendary city that's still  practically deserted two years after  Islamist insurgents fled hai dharam alia  has stayed he's one of the city's unsung  heroes he risked his life to save  cultural treasures from the terrorists  for five generations now Malaya's family  has run a library in Timbuktu it housed  and preserved priceless manuscripts some  dating back almost a thousand years but  two years ago things changed since the  jihadi fighters marched into the city  the shelves have been filled with  nothing but desert sand people used to  come here every morning to study or read  there were desks over there and the  shelves were full but then the gunmen  came and put an end to all that but  Malaya wasn't going to let the city's  cultural heritage be destroyed without a  fight he hid manuscripts down the legs  of his trousers and in his pockets and  smuggled them back to his house he even  removed the sign outside the library and  took it home with him to avoid any  suspicion  since then he's been storing some three  thousand manuscripts at home some of the  texts were written by Islamic scholars  others are works about science and  geography Malaya has seven crates of  them I'm the eldest in my family's  that's why it's my responsibility I'm  doing things the way my ancestors  prescribed I'm not allowed to sell them  to anyone I can't even give him away in  a baboon attack okay it goes without  saying that he wanted to stop this  legacy falling into the hands of the  terrorists who arrived in early 2012 al  Qaeda in Maghreb Mirage a militia and  the Malian group Ansar Dine seized large  parts of northern Mali back then at  first they fought alongside to our egg  rebels but later turned against them  Malian troops were only able to repel  the Islamists with the help of the  French army this exclusive footage taken  by a Malian cameraman shows the cruelty  of the rampaging terrorists  the damage that the islamists inflicted  on the culture of the region is perhaps  most clearly visible at the ahmed baba  institute in the heart of Timbuktu here  the jihad is burned more than four  thousand manuscripts one of hadera  Malays friends is the deputy director  here two years after the attack he's  still coming to terms with it since it  is if a neck and neck do it had nothing  to do with the ideology the Islamists  were here for 10 months and didn't burn  anything they only destroyed the  manuscripts the night they fled they did  it to spite the world because they knew  their cultural and scientific value they  knew just how important they were their  intention was to anger Timbuktu Mali and  the international community even though  hai dharam allah's manuscript collection  hardly earns him any money and he  struggles to feed his family he's proud  that he has done what his ancestors  would have wanted saved every single one  of the unique cultural treasures for  posterity
 

